{"text":"{
  "status": "STABLE",
  "core_findings": [
    "Masked-target separation is broadly healthy: negative items beat positives at only 4.48–6.88% of probed positions. Electronics and Industrial_and_Scientific have weaker lower-tail margins (0.5434 and 0.624), indicating more difficult cases.",
    "Item representations retain substantial dimensional spread (RankMe 48.88–50.69 of 64); appended-MASK user representations are more concentrated (19.56–26.27). Directional alignment alone does not establish collapse or recommendation diversity."
  ],
  "key_implications": [
    "The model usually separates observed masked targets from sampled negatives, although these probes do not establish candidate-ranking HR@10.",
    "User representations occupy fewer effective dimensions than item embeddings, especially in Industrial_and_Scientific.",
    "Training-allocation interpretation is limited by inconsistent documentation: snapshots report fixed 64/64 branches, whereas definitions describe Bernoulli allocation."
  ],
  "evidence": {
    "headline_metrics": {
      "Electronics:pos_neg_margin_p10": 0.5434,
      "Industrial_and_Scientific:pos_neg_neg_beats_pos_rate": 0.0688,
      "Industrial_and_Scientific:real_items_rankme": 48.875,
      "Industrial_and_Scientific:appended_mask_users_rankme": 19.557
    },
    "brief_metric_read": {
      "pos_neg_margin_p10": "Positive but relatively weak lower-tail separation in Electronics.",
      "pos_neg_neg_beats_pos_rate": "Even the highest category rate remains below 7%.",
      "representation_geometry": "Centered spectra show dimensional spread, with greater concentration among user representations."
    }
  }
}"}
